WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.000
A coding agent changed an admin API. Its narrow session test still passed, but an anonymous caller could now receive an admin-shaped response. This is the false confidence CodeDecay is built to challenge.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:27.196
The demo uses a dependency-free, two-commit repository generated by the public setup script. This is one genuine Codex session in a workspace-write sandbox. Private paths are redacted, and only waiting and repetitive output are cut.

00:00:27.196 --> 00:00:44.240
Before Codex edits anything, CodeDecay packages the diff, repository memory, the API review skill, and the approved unit check. It reports high risk, two changed security-sensitive files, and missing real-path proof, even though the configured unit test passed.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:58.240
Codex reads the bundle and the repository skill, then makes a four-step plan: inspect the real path, reproduce both caller states, add a red endpoint test before changing production code, and only then repair and revalidate.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:14.445
The live probe reaches the actual Node HTTP server and returns two hundred with an anonymous admin. Codex adds an endpoint test without mocking the route. Before the repair, the anonymous assertion fails two hundred versus four oh one, while the authorized case remains green.

00:01:14.445 --> 00:01:29.445
Codex restores fail-closed behavior at both layers. Missing authorization no longer creates a session, the users route denies a missing session, and the real endpoint test becomes a named, explicitly configured CodeDecay check.

00:01:29.445 --> 00:01:44.445
After the repair, the original unit test passes, both anonymous and authorized endpoint cases pass, the live probe returns four oh one with an unauthorized body, and the diff is clean. CodeDecay independently executes both allowlisted checks again.

00:01:44.445 --> 00:02:01.445
Revalidation marks the missing-nearby-test finding fixed, and missing-test findings drop to zero. But CodeDecay still reports high risk at eighty, confirms four auth and API context signals, and says proof remains static without a runtime coverage artifact.

00:02:01.445 --> 00:02:19.551
Codex self-reviews the result. The HTTP behavior and checks are tool evidence. JWT hardening, fuzzing, and mutation testing are suggestions, not executed proof. The fixture still accepts any non-empty token as admin, so neither Codex nor CodeDecay claims zero risk.

00:02:19.551 --> 00:02:39.320
Try the public Judge Lab with no login, inspect the open-source repository and the twenty-three of twenty-three planted-issue benchmark, or install at submux h q slash codedecay. The tagged release is version zero point four point one. The exact fixture, transcript, commands, captions, and timing-cut disclosure are public.
